<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/867">867</a>–<a href="/years/933">933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Li Siyuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang, Li Siyuan, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> general and adopted son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> who took the throne in 926 after the death of Emperor Zhuangzong. His reign restored a measure of order to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and under his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-dao">Feng Dao</a> work began in 932 on the first printed edition of the Confucian classics. His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a> founded Later Jin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 清善成堂刻本 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%90%E6%98%8E%E5%AE%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Siyuan was born on 10 October 867 in Yingzhou, in present-day Ying County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> name Miaojilie and no surname, like his nomadic Turkic ancestors. His father, known in Chinese histories as Li Ni, was a general under the Shatuo leader Li Guochang, and his mother was a Lady Liu. After his father's death in 879, Li Guochang took the boy as a bodyguard, impressed by his mounted archery; it was said he never missed a bird in flight. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, Li Guochang's son, later adopted the quiet youth and gave him the Chinese name Li Siyuan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 11 June 884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> attended a feast at Bian Prefecture given by the military governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, whose assassins attacked the drunken guest in his lodging that night. The 16-year-old Li Siyuan helped his master over a low wall, and the two escaped unharmed in a thunderstorm while over 300 attendants were killed. He was then given command of Li Keyong's bodyguard cavalry.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>'s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> he led assaults against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, served as a battlefield commander in the Jin victory at Boxiang, and in 923 crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> with 5,000 elite soldiers to take Yun Prefecture by surprise, becoming military governor of Tianping. Rumours about his loyalty spread at court, and he survived them through the protection of Li Shaohong, director of palace affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Li Siyuan seized power in 926 after the mutiny known as the Xingjiao Gate Incident killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, and he made An Chonghui his chief adviser. Because the emperor was illiterate and An could not follow every memorial, the imperial scholars of Duanming Hall were created to read submissions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-dao">Feng Dao</a> and Zhao Feng as the first. To lighten the naming taboo he renamed himself Li Dan in 927. Despite many natural disasters, his reign was markedly more peaceful than the half-century before it.</p><p>On 5 December 933 he fell seriously ill after a trip in the snow. His eldest son Li Congrong, fearing the throne would go to his younger brother Li Conghou, attacked the palace gates. The dying emperor wept and told his men to deal with the matter themselves. He died on 15 December 933.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Siyuan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shi Jingtang, a Shatuo general and son-in-law of <b>Emperor Mingzong</b> of the Later Tang, rebelled at Taiyuan in 936.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin (936-947)</a></li><li><blockquote>His adopted brother <b>Li Siyuan (Emperor Mingzong)</b> restored order, and under his minister Feng Dao work began in 932 on the first printed edition of the Confucian classics.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yingzhou. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=909" aria-label="Luoyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4469 4584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4469 4584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=909">What was here in 909 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
